<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Megiddo</h1><p class="st-meta">2000 BCE – 1150 BCE (approx.) · capital Megiddo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Megiddo, a fortified Canaanite city guarding a key pass into the Jezreel Valley, scene of Thutmose III's famous victory and origin of the name Armageddon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> had been a walled town since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> kingdom, rebuilt with massive ramparts and a palace in the early 2nd millennium BCE, controlled the route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Carmel</a> ridge along which armies and caravans travelled between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled from a palace near the city gate, alongside a temple to Canaanite gods. In c. 1457 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom</a> defeated a coalition led by the king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kadesh">Kadesh</a> outside the city and besieged it for seven months. Afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> was an Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>; its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biridiya">Biridiya</a> wrote several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Canaanite city was destroyed in the 12th century BCE as Egyptian power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> collapsed. A later town became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, and the hoard of carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a> survives from its last Canaanite palace.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 2000 BCE – 1150 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:19.90%;width:16.92%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2000 BCE – 1150 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Megiddo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Biridiya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Canaanite religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Canaanite, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Canaanite Megiddo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1575&amp;focus=megiddo" aria-label="Megiddo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5583 994 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Megiddo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5903 1250L5863 1489"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5863 1489h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5903 1250h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5903 1250h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Megiddo. <a href="/atlas?year=-1575&amp;focus=megiddo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

